  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Mild and quiet weather expected through Wednesday, though\n   morning low clouds or valley fog is possible.\n\n-  Two weak systems bring showers Thursday and Friday with light\n   dusting of snow above 5,000 feet, and westerly breezes. \n\n-  Mild and dry conditions return for the weekend, with morning\n   fog remaining possible. \n\nBreezy northwest winds along the Continental Divide (including \ngusts over 20 mph near Butte) will gradually ease today as high \npressure builds into the Northern Rockies. The combination of \nrecent rainfall and calming winds has trapped low clouds and\npatchy fog in the valleys this morning, including across portions\nof north-central Idaho. Expect these low clouds to slowly break \nup and give way to some sunshine by this afternoon.\n\nPatchy fog is could redevelop in the valleys tonight. Tuesday and\nWednesday will remain generally mild and quiet. Despite some \nincreasing high clouds on Tuesday, afternoon temperatures will run\na few degrees above normal, reaching the 40s and 50s in the lower\nvalleys of Idaho. Wednesday may feature more sunshine as stable \nconditions persist.\n\nA weak weather system will move through the region on Thursday, \nbringing light snow showers primarily to elevations above 5,000 \nfeet. Showers will linger into Friday, particularly across \nnorthwest Montana and the Glacier National Park region, \naccompanied by an uptick in westerly breezes.\n\nHeading into next weekend, high pressure builds right back over \nthe region. This will bring a return to dry and mild afternoons, \nthough the quiet pattern is favorable for morning fog in the \nvalleys once again.",
